  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=1+2(x-2)+2(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(x-2)^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{\u2034}(2)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x-2)^{3}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(2)}{3!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(2)}{3!} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(2)}{6} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
